diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1c304f6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,221 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Members manage their own bots at `/bots`, reached through **Set up chat bots**
+in account settings. Administrators can manage every non-deleted bot. The list is
+unpaginated, and each bot's code is a secret bearer credential.
+
+Bots use the existing User model with the `bot` role, a creator, and a random
+32-character code protected by a unique database index. Unlike people, bots do
+not join open rooms by default. **Add to all open rooms** is an opt-in checkbox
+on both bot forms. Enabling it joins every existing open room and automatically
+joins future open rooms (including closed rooms converted to open). Disabling
+it keeps current memberships and stops future automatic additions. Existing
+bots also default to off; their existing memberships are preserved.
+
+Room managers can select individual bots using the usual room access switches
+in open or closed room settings. In open rooms, automatically joining bots are
+shown as checked and disabled; turn off the bot's setting to manage those
+memberships individually. People still join all open rooms automatically.
+Bots cannot sign in through the password login form or be promoted through the
+account's people controls.
+
+Deleting a bot deactivates it, removes all memberships, and clears its code.
+Existing messages keep their author, consistent with removing a human account.
+
+## Posting
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/ROOM_ID/BOT_CODE/messages
+```
+
+The request body is UTF-8 text, not JSON or a form. Even curl's default
+`application/x-www-form-urlencoded` content type is treated as literal text.
+For files or multiline output, use `--data-binary @file.txt`. HTML is escaped,
+not interpreted. The endpoint does not accept a multipart attachment envelope.
+
+Responses:
+
+- `201`, with `Location: /messages/MESSAGE_ID` after creation and broadcast
+- `422` for empty/whitespace-only or invalid UTF-8 text
+- `404` for an unknown room or missing bot membership
+- `401` for an invalid, deleted, or inactive bot's credential
+
+The endpoint uses only the path credential, never a browser session. CSRF
+protection remains enabled for management pages and human message endpoints.
+Message creation uses the normal room notification callbacks and broadcasts.
+
+## Credential handling
+
+Do not share bot codes or embed them in public scripts. Use a CI secret store
+and HTTPS. The management list is marked `no-store` and excluded from Turbo's
+snapshot cache. Rails filters `bot_code` parameters and the code segment in its
+filtered request paths. A small middleware prevents request bodies from being
+parsed or logged as form parameters before the controller runs.
+
+**Deployment:** configure reverse proxies, access logs, tracing, and external
+error reporting to redact `/rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` URLs as well.
+Application-level Rails filtering cannot protect logs maintained outside Rails.
+
+## Regression checks
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/bots/messages_controller_test.rb test/models/user/bot_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+Set **Webhook URL** on either bot form to receive mention callbacks. It is
+optional; saving an empty field removes it. The URL must be HTTP(S), with no
+embedded username/password or fragment. HTTPS is recommended.
+
+Bots appear in the same mention autocomplete as people in rooms they belong to.
+A newly created human-authored message queues one `Message::BotWebhookJob` for
+each distinct active, mentioned member bot with a webhook. Neither DNS nor HTTP
+runs in the message-posting request. Production uses the existing Resque workers;
+development uses Rails' asynchronous adapter. Edits and bot-authored messages do
+not trigger callbacks (the latter prevents bot reply loops).
+
+Each callback is an HTTP POST with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Only the addressed bot's mention attachments are removed from `body.text`;
+other mentions and ordinary text remain. `body.html` retains the full rendered
+rich text. Paths are relative to the Campfire origin. **Treat `room.reply_to` as a
+secret:** it contains this bot's posting credential. It can be used later with
+the raw-text posting API described above.
+
+To reply immediately, return `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html`
+(optional charset parameters are supported) and a UTF-8 response body. Plain
+text is escaped with line breaks preserved; HTML goes through the normal Action
+Text rendering/sanitization. The reply is created and broadcast as the bot and
+uses the usual room notifications. Return `204` for no reply. Other statuses,
+unsupported content types, empty bodies, invalid UTF-8, and oversized replies
+are ignored.
+
+Delivery uses the existing public-address restriction, pins the resolved IP,
+and does not use proxies or follow redirects. Local/private network endpoints
+are blocked; use a publicly reachable endpoint when developing integrations.
+Connection timeout is 5 seconds, read/write timeouts are 10 seconds, and replies
+are limited to 1 MiB. Failures do not affect the human's message and are not
+automatically retried. Membership, active status, and webhook presence are
+checked at execution time and again before posting a response. Deleted source
+records are discarded by the job.
+
+Additional regression checks:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/message/bot_webhook_test.rb \
+  test/jobs/message/bot_webhook_job_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/autocompletable/users_controller_test.rb
+```
+
+## Joined webhooks
+
+The same optional **Webhook URL** receives a greeting event when a bot is first
+added to a non-direct room, whether by a person or automatically:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+There is no `author` or `message` in this event. Direct conversations never send
+`joined` (they still support mentions). A `200` text/plain or text/html response
+posts a greeting as the bot using exactly the same response handling, broadcast,
+notifications, size limits, and HTTP safeguards as mention callbacks.
+
+`Membership` records a unique `Room::BotJoin` for each bot/room pair in the
+membership transaction. Its after-commit callback enqueues `Room::BotJoinJob`.
+Bot grants use callback-aware creation; people retain bulk insertion. Repeated
+grants, edits, toggling automatic membership, and removal/re-addition do not
+enqueue another greeting for that room. The join record outlives membership
+removal, but is deleted with its room or user. Rollbacks enqueue nothing.
+
+As with mentions, delivery is not retried on HTTP failure and is skipped for an
+inactive bot, missing webhook, or revoked room access. Adding a webhook later
+does not replay old joins, and migration does not send greetings for existing
+memberships. The uniqueness record prevents duplicate scheduling; it does not
+promise successful exactly-once delivery over an unreliable network.
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_test.rb test/models/room/bot_join_test.rb \
+  test/jobs/room/bot_join_job_test.rb test/controllers/rooms/opens_controller_test.rb
+```
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators can set **Bots per member** in Account settings. The default is
+5, including for existing accounts without this JSON setting. Zero disables new
+member-owned bots; administrators are exempt. The limit counts each creator's
+non-deactivated bots (including banned bots), not everyone else's bots. Deleting
+a bot frees a slot.
+
+Lowering the limit never removes bots or prevents editing/deleting them. The
+bots page hides **Add a chat bot** at or above the limit. The new-bot route also
+refuses access, and creation validation rejects direct submissions or stale
+forms. The setting is stored with the existing typed account settings, so no
+migration is required.
+
+Regression checks for limits:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_limit_test.rb test/models/account_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/accounts_controller_test.rb test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
+
+## Automatic bot safeguards
+
+These limits are fixed in `User::Bot::Safeguards`, not account settings:
+
+- A bot can post ten messages in a rolling ten-second window across all rooms.
+  API messages and both mention/join webhook replies share the same limit.
+- The next message is rejected and starts a thirty-second cooldown. API requests
+  return `429` with a rounded-up `Retry-After` in seconds. Webhook replies are
+  discarded, not retried. Five further posting attempts in that cooldown suspend
+  the bot; the fifth returns `403`. Expiring a cooldown resets its attempt count.
+- Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries suspend a bot. Non-2xx responses,
+  timeouts, connection errors, and blocked/unresolvable destinations are failures.
+  Every completed 2xx delivery resets the streak, even without a usable reply.
+
+The bounded rolling timestamp list and counters are persisted on the bot user.
+`with_lock` serializes state changes and message creation across processes (the
+SQLite configuration uses immediate transactions). HTTP requests are made outside
+that lock. Webhook delivery reloads the bot before sending, and replies check
+posting permission again: already in-flight requests cannot be recalled, but a
+suspended bot cannot post their replies. New and queued deliveries skip suspended
+bots. Failed delivery accounting is shared by join and mention webhooks.
+
+Suspension is a distinct user status, not deactivation or banning: credentials,
+rooms, and messages remain intact, and the bot still uses its owner's capacity.
+Owners and administrators see **Suspended** and its reason on the bots page.
+Only administrators see **Reactivate**, protected server-side and using Turbo's
+native browser confirmation. Reactivation clears all safeguard state so the bot
+starts fresh. Editing a bot or waiting does not lift suspension.
+
+Regression checks:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots test/models/user/bot
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
diff --git a/lib/bot_requests.rb b/lib/bot_requests.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c40f59e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/bot_requests.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Bot messages carry plain text even when curl supplies a form content type.
+# Prevent Rails' instrumentation (which runs before the controller) from parsing
+# or logging that text as request parameters.
+class BotRequests
+  PATH = %r{(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages(?:\.[^/?]+)?/?)(?=\?|\z)}
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if PATH.match?(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      env["action_dispatch.request.request_parameters"] = {}
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+
+  # Parameter filters alone do not redact credentials in URL path segments.
+  module FilteredPath
+    def filtered_path
+      super.sub(PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+    end
+  end
+end
